 How To Report Phishing in Botswana

If you get a phishing message, do not ignore it. Report it. Reporting helps protect you and other students.

 Step 1: Do NOT Click

- Do NOT click the link
- Do NOT reply to the message
- Do NOT send money, password, PIN, or OTP

 Step 2: Take a Screenshot

- Take a screenshot of the message
- Keep the phone number or email of the sender
- Do NOT delete the message yet

 Step 3: Tell Your School

Tell one of these people at your school:

- Your lecturer
- Your IT Department / Help Desk
- Your Student Support Office

Show them the screenshot and tell them what happened.

 Step 4: Tell Your Bank (If It Is About Money)

If the message talks about your bank account:

- Call your bank yourself on the official number on your bank card
- Do NOT call the number in the fake message
- Tell them you got a phishing message
- Ask them to check if your account is safe

 Step 5: Report To The Police

If you lost money or shared your password:

1. Go to the nearest Police Station
2. Tell them you got a phishing message
3. Show them the screenshot
4. Ask for a case number

You can also report to the Botswana Police on 999 in an emergency.

 Step 6: Change Your Password

If you clicked the link or shared your password:

1. Change your password right now
2. Turn on Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)
3. Change the password for your email too
4. Tell your bank

 What To Say When You Report

You can copy this and send it:

> Hello, I got a phishing message today.
> Here is a screenshot.
> The sender number / email was: [put it here]
> I did NOT click the link / I clicked the link by mistake.
> Please help me check if my account is safe.

 Remember

Reporting is not embarrassing. 
Even smart people get phishing messages.
Reporting helps stop the criminals.
